# Source: Exercise sheet "hoare logic 3", division loop (after item m); slides "Recipe for Loop Invariants - tips" (integer division).
id: integer-division
title: Integer division by repeated subtraction
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: Exercise sheet "hoare logic 3"; Slides 7, tips
tags: while, invariant, division, variant

task:
  Prove that the loop computes the quotient $q$ and the remainder $r$ of
  the division of $x'$ by $y'$. The variables $x'$ and $y'$ are logical
  variables: they keep the initial values of $x$ and $y$. Then give a
  variant and show that the loop terminates.

  The precondition contains $x' >= 0$. Without it the triple is not valid:
  from $x' = -1$ the loop does not run, and $r = -1$ breaks $0 <= r$.

given:
  Loop condition B: $r >= y$

pre: x = x' && y = y' && y' > 0 && x' >= 0 && q = 0 && r = x
post: x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r < y'

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (r >= y) do {
    {I && r >= y}
    {R2}
    r := r - y;      // b1
    {R1}
    q := q + 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(r >= y)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  Every iteration moves one copy of $y$ from $r$ into the quotient, so
  $q * y' + r$ keeps the value $x'$. The remainder never becomes negative,
  since the body runs only when $r >= y$. The variable $y$ is never
  assigned, so $y = y'$ and $y' > 0$ from $P$ stay true. We keep them in
  $I$: $y = y'$ is needed in preservation and at the exit, and $y' > 0$
  is needed for termination.
set: I = x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $r >= y$: initialisation $P ==> I$, preservation of
  $I$ by the body, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. No statement comes
  before the loop, so the precondition $P$ must imply $I$.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: x = x' && y = y' && y' > 0 && x' >= 0 && q = 0 && r = x ==> I
why:
  From $q = 0$ and $r = x = x'$: $q * y' + r = 0 * y' + x' = x'$, and
  $0 <= r$ follows from $x' >= 0$. The facts $y = y'$ and $y' > 0$ are in
  $P$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of q := q + 1 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «r >= y : B»} r := r - y; q := q + 1 {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $q$ with $q + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(q := q + 1, I)
  = (x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0)[q <- q + 1]     // I
  = x' = (q + 1) * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0      // q is replaced by q + 1
set: R1 = x' = (q + 1) * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0

step: R2, the weakest precondition of r := r - y for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $r$ with $r - y$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(r := r - y, R1)
  = (x' = (q + 1) * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0)[r <- r - y]     // R1
  = x' = (q + 1) * y' + (r - y) && 0 <= r - y && y = y' && y' > 0    // r is replaced by r - y
  = x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r - y && y = y' && y' > 0                // with y = y', (q + 1) * y' + (r - y) is q * y' + r
  = x' = q * y' + r && y <= r && y = y' && y' > 0                    // add y to both sides of 0 <= r - y
set: R2 = x' = q * y' + r && y <= r && y = y' && y' > 0

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && r >= y, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «r >= y : B» ==> «R2»
vc: x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0 && r >= y ==> x' = q * y' + r && y <= r && y = y' && y' > 0
why:
  $y <= r$ is $B$. The other three parts of $R2$ are part of $I$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(r >= y), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(r >= y) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0 && !(r >= y) ==> x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r < y'
why:
  $!(r >= y)$ is $r < y$, and $y = y'$ gives $r < y'$. The equation and
  $0 <= r$ are part of $I$.

step: The variant is not negative
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: I && r >= y
text:
  The body decreases $r$ by $y$, which is positive, and $r$ is never
  negative. So we take the variant $E = r$. The rule WHILE-TOTAL has two
  premises. The first one: $E$ is not negative when an iteration starts.
  >>> «I» && «r >= y : B» ==> «r >= 0 : E >= 0»
vc: x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0 && r >= y ==> r >= 0
why:
  $0 <= r$ is part of $I$.

step: The weakest precondition of q := q + 1 for r < E0
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  The second premise: the body keeps $I$ and makes $E$ smaller. $E0$ is a
  logical variable for the value of $E$ when the iteration starts. The
  body keeps $I$ by the preservation premise, so it remains to show
  $r < E0$ at the end of the body:
  >>> {«I» && «r >= y : B» && «r = E0 : E = E0»} r := r - y; q := q + 1 {«r < E0 : E < E0»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $r < E0$, from
  the last statement. We substitute $q$ with $q + 1$.
calc:
  wp(q := q + 1, r < E0)
  = (r < E0)[q <- q + 1]     // E is r
  = r < E0                   // q does not occur in r < E0, so nothing changes

step: The body decreases the variant
part: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $r$ with $r - y$ in $r < E0$, computed in the previous
  step. Then the precondition of the triple must imply the result.
calc:
  wp(r := r - y, r < E0)
  = (r < E0)[r <- r - y]     // the result of the previous step
  = r - y < E0               // r is replaced by r - y
vc: x' = q * y' + r && 0 <= r && y = y' && y' > 0 && r >= y && r = E0 ==> r - y < E0
why:
  From $y = y'$ and $y' > 0$ we get $y > 0$, so $r - y < r$, and $r = E0$.
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.
